---
/**
 * Plan comparison table (rendered by pricing.astro under the plan cards).
 *
 * The layout is the "hero column" comparison style: one rounded panel, features
 * down the left, plans across the top, and the FEATURED plan carried in a tinted
 * column the eye can follow without hunting. `highlight` decides which column
 * that is — the same flag the card grid uses for its badge — so the two surfaces
 * cannot disagree about which plan is featured.
 *
 * Three structural decisions, each with its reason:
 *
 *  - FULL HEIGHT; ONLY THE WIDTH SCROLLS (`overflow-x-auto`, no `max-height`).
 *    The panel used to be capped at 44rem with `overflow-auto`, which put a
 *    scrollbar inside the table and hid the last rows behind it until the reader
 *    found it. The table now grows to its content and the PAGE does the vertical
 *    scrolling.
 *
 *    The cost is stated because it is not obvious: with the panel as a
 *    scrollport, `sticky top-0` on the header row no longer sticks — measured on
 *    the built page, the row scrolls away with the page. The spec promotes
 *    `overflow-y` to `auto` beside any `overflow-x`, so the panel remains the
 *    nearest scrollport even when it has nothing to scroll vertically, and a
 *    pinned child is trapped inside it. What still earns its keep is
 *    `sticky left-0` on the feature column: on a narrow screen the panel IS a
 *    real horizontal scroller, and without that pin a reader scrolling out to
 *    the Enterprise column loses the row label entirely. `tabindex="0"` keeps
 *    that region keyboard-scrollable — a scroll region a keyboard user cannot
 *    reach is a region they cannot read.
 *
 *  - THE TINT IS A TOKEN (`bg-plan-tint`, global.css), never a translucent wash
 *    of the primary colour. The tinted header and the tinted cells are sticky,
 *    and a see-through background lets the rows scrolling underneath show
 *    through the pinned cell. One semantic value per theme also keeps the site
 *    rule that components consume tokens and never raw hex.
 *
 *  - CELL VALUES ARE DATA (`boolean | string | number`). A boolean renders a
 *    glyph PLUS an `sr-only` word; the plan cards spell inclusion with an
 *    `aria-hidden` glyph and nothing else, which tells a screen-reader user the
 *    feature but not whether it is included. The glyph keeps the card grid's own
 *    vocabulary (`text-link` for included, `text-muted/50` for not) instead of a
 *    second palette of its own.
 *
 * Nothing here is hardcoded English: the heading, the chip, the price-row label,
 * the currency note and both inclusion words come from the i18n dictionary, and
 * every figure comes from the tier objects the cards above were rendered from —
 * so this table cannot advertise a price the checkout does not bill.
 */
import type { FeatureRow, PricingTier } from '../content/pricing/types';
import { t } from '../i18n';

interface Props {
  rows: FeatureRow[];
  /** The full tiers, not just their names: header and price row both read prices. */
  columns: PricingTier[];
}

const { rows, columns } = Astro.props;
const locale = Astro.currentLocale ?? 'en';
const comparisonLabel = t(locale, 'pricingPage.comparison');
// Tiers with a real price id: the two-month note is a yearly-billing statement
// and telling it is simply false for the free-forever and quote-only tiers.
const BILLED_TIERS = new Set(['plus', 'pro', 'premium']);
---

<section aria-labelledby="plan-comparison">
  <h2 id="plan-comparison" class="text-center text-2xl font-bold">{comparisonLabel}</h2>

  <div
    class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-ink/10 bg-surface"
    tabindex="0"
    role="group"
    aria-label={comparisonLabel}
  >
    <table class="w-full min-w-[46rem] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <caption class="sr-only">{comparisonLabel}</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          {/* The corner cell names the axis. Left blank it is an unnamed header
              cell a screen reader reads as empty, and this is also where the
              reference layout puts the word for the row dimension. */}
          <th
            scope="col"
            class="sticky left-0 z-30 border-b border-ink/10 bg-surface px-5 py-5 align-bottom text-xs font-normal text-muted"
          >
            {t(locale, 'pricingPage.feature')}
          </th>
          {
            columns.map((c) => (
              <th
                scope="col"
                class:list={[
                  'border-b border-ink/10 px-5 py-5 align-bottom',
                  c.highlight ? 'bg-plan-tint' : 'bg-surface',
                ]}
              >
                {/* No "Most Popular" chip here: the tinted column says it
                    without a second badge, three inches under a card that
                    already carries that badge. */}
                <span class="block text-base font-bold text-ink">{c.name}</span>
                <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs font-normal text-muted">
                  {c.prices.yearly.price}
                  {c.prices.yearly.period && ` ${c.prices.yearly.period}`}
                </span>
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th
                scope="row"
                class="sticky left-0 z-10 border-b border-ink/5 bg-surface px-5 py-4 text-left align-top text-sm font-normal text-muted"
              >
                {row.label}
              </th>
              {columns.map((c) => (
                <td
                  class:list={[
                    'border-b border-ink/5 px-5 py-4 align-top',
                    c.highlight ? 'bg-plan-tint' : '',
                  ]}
                >
                  {typeof row.values[c.tierKey] === 'boolean' ? (
                    row.values[c.tierKey] === true ? (
                      <>
                        <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-base font-semibold text-link">
                          ✓
                        </span>
                        <span class="sr-only">{t(locale, 'pricingPage.included')}</span>
                      </>
                    ) : (
                      <>
                        <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-base text-muted/70">
                          ✗
                        </span>
                        <span class="sr-only">{t(locale, 'pricingPage.notIncluded')}</span>
                      </>
                    )
                  ) : (
                    <span class="font-medium text-ink">{row.values[c.tierKey]}</span>
                  )}
                </td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
      {/* The price row is the LAST row of the table, inside the tinted column
          like every other row, so the featured plan's figure is where a reader
          who scrolled the whole table lands. The monthly figure is the primary
          one because it is the smaller number; the currency and the yearly
          statement sit under it as the qualifier rather than as a second price
          competing with the first. */}
      <tfoot>
        <tr>
          <th
            scope="row"
            class="sticky left-0 z-10 bg-surface px-5 py-5 text-left align-top text-sm font-normal text-muted"
          >
            {t(locale, 'pricingPage.planPricing')}
          </th>
          {
            columns.map((c) => (
              <td class:list={['px-5 py-5 align-top', c.highlight ? 'bg-plan-tint' : '']}>
                <span class="block font-semibold text-ink">
                  {c.prices.monthly.price}
                  {c.prices.monthly.period && (
                    <span class="text-xs font-normal text-muted"> {c.prices.monthly.period}</span>
                  )}
                </span>
                <span class="mt-1 block text-xs text-muted">
                  {c.currency}
                  {BILLED_TIERS.has(c.tierKey) && ` · ${t(locale, 'pricingPage.twoMonthsFree')}`}
                </span>
              </td>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </tfoot>
    </table>
  </div>

  {/* One footnote element — a single paragraph, so the small print under the
      table is one thing to read rather than two blocks that happen to be
      adjacent. `<br />` separates the four notes because they are one voice:
      the asterisks' legend, one line per marked row, then the perpetual-
      licensing note that carries no marker of its own. (A `<ul>` plus a `<p>`,
      the previous shape, drew a list-marker gutter and a second paragraph
      rhythm for lines that are all the same kind of aside.)

      Faint muted and 10px — the smallest text on the page — because this is
      reference material a reader consults after the prices, not a claim
      competing with them. It is the token at alpha rather than an `opacity-50`
      on the box: the colour token is this site's existing way to say
      "de-emphasised" (the ✗ glyph above uses it too), and it cannot leave a
      half-transparent background behind when a parent gains one.

      THE 80% IS A DESIGN CALL, NOT AN AA PASS — know the number before changing
      it. Measured by painting the computed colour over each theme's real page
      background and running the WCAG relative-luminance formula, 80% reads
      dark 4.26:1 / light 4.62:1 at this size: the light theme clears the 4.5:1
      WCAG AA floor for normal text and the DARK THEME DOES NOT. That is the
      owner's ruling of 2026-09-29 — the note is an aside under the prices and
      should read as one — so the exposure is stated here rather than quietly
      absorbed. `text-muted/84` is the faintest value that passes in both themes
      (dark 4.53:1 / light 5.12:1) and 83% is where dark fails at 4.45:1, so if
      legibility outranks the tint, 84 is the number, not 100. Whole-token
      `text-muted` would read 5.96:1 dark / 7.76:1 light, but it is the weight of
      the table's own column labels — the note has to sit below those, not
      beside them. Re-measure with that method, not by eye.

      Outside the scrolling panel so it stays visible while the reader scrolls
      the table, and inside the section so it travels with the table it
      annotates. One line per marked row: all three payment rows share the `*`,
      so each line names its own subject — a single note would silently orphan
      two markers, and the rows' own order is the only thing tying a line back
      to its row. */}
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-[10px] leading-relaxed text-muted/80">
    {t(locale, 'pricingPage.qrisNote')}<br />
    {t(locale, 'pricingPage.midtransNote')}<br />
    {t(locale, 'pricingPage.stripeNote')}<br />
    {t(locale, 'pricingPage.note')}
  </p>
</section>
